Pallasite Reel
Ingredients: BLUEGRASS + CELESTIAL, SPACE & ORBITAL + Marimba
Creative Catalyst: Acoustic instruments are hand-forged from iron-nickel meteorite slabs and geared with escapements salvaged from 19th-century church tower clocks.

Pallasite Reel sounds like a hyper-caffeinated mountain porch jam hurled into geostationary orbit. Blistering, Scruggs-style bluegrass pickings and high-lonesome three-part harmonies interlock with the hollow, vitreous clatter of a heavy-iron marimba whose struck bars chime like cold bells across a vacuum. Clockwork escapements rattle beneath the tempo, injecting erratic micro-hesitations into galloping hoedown rhythms before plunging into vast, airless gravitational decays.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 135–165 BPM. Ferocious traditional Appalachian cut-time (2/4 and 4/4) driven by clawhammer attacks, disrupted by the mechanical slip-and-catch of clockwork pendulums that generate sudden micro-rubato slingshots.
- Harmonic Palette: Cold modal open tunings (Modal D, Open C, saw-mill tunings) blended with Lydian and Mixolydian extensions; eerie celestial suspensions that hover between lonesome mountain laments and astronomical radio beacons.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Escapement-Lathe Dopplering: Microphone arrays are mounted onto swinging clock pendulums inside an evacuated chamber, generating authentic mechanical pitch-wobble and phasing without digital LFOs.
  2. Vacuum Slapback Chambering: Dry DI signals run through zero-reverberation convolution profiles of satellite telemetry clean-rooms, creating unnervingly dry, airless transients that decay abruptly.
  3. Chondritic Lattice Resonators: Notch filtering calibrated precisely to the resonance frequencies of olivine peridot crystals embedded in pallasite iron, amplifying high glassy overtones whenever marimba bars or resonator banjo plates are struck.
- Dynamics: Violent contrasts between blinding, all-hands bluegrass breakdowns and breathless orbital drift zones where all rhythm drops away, leaving only a solitary clockwork spring ticking against a deep planetary sub-drone.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement materialized within the Radio Quiet Zone of Pocahontas County, West Virginia, where rogue astronomers and multi-generational fiddle makers hollowed out an abandoned greenhouse observatory adjacent to the Green Bank dishes. Drawing heat from woodstoves and light from sodium-vapor nursery fixtures, these craftspeople melted iron-nickel fragments recovered from historic bolide strikes, casting them into heavy marimba tone-bars and machining antique brass church-clock escapements into banjo heads. The subculture—known locally as "Tickers" or "Spore-Pickers"—dresses in grease-stained heavy duck overalls over high-collared cambric shirts, wearing jeweler’s loupes and unlacquered meteoric iron fingerpicks that oxidize into green halos around their knuckles. Their gatherings take place during lunar apogees, where dancers stomp on cedar planks while musicians push the tempo to match the calculated velocity of falling orbital debris.

Typical Instruments
- Pallasite-Bar Marimba: Primary melodic anchor; thick bars forged from extraterrestrial olivine-iron suspended over tuned brass chime-pipes.
- Escapement 5-String Banjo: Fitted with an antique clockwork gear-train in the pot that mechanically oscillates the fifth string drone.
- Chondrite Fiddle: Built with an aerolith bridge and stranded aerospace-alloy wire strings that produce a biting, bell-like timbre.
- Pre-War Dreadnought Acoustic: Strung with heavy-gauge bronze, flatpicked with jagged stone plectrums.
- Orbital Chamber Synthesizer: Custom analog synth that emits ultra-low sub-harmonic drone beds modeling planetary gravitational wells.

